This product includes mathematics and derived code from:

  Easing Equations — Robert Penner, "Easing Equations v1.5", 1 May 2003
  (https://robertpenner.com/easing/penner_easing_as1.txt), released under the MIT and
  3-Clause BSD licences. The full notices are in LICENSE-PENNER.txt, which ships with
  the package.

  Where it appears: src/formats/eases.js names the closed ease table; src/ease-bake.js
  implements the curves and samples them into CSS `linear()`. quad/cubic/quart/quint
  (and their `power1`-`power4` aliases), sine, exponential, circular, elastic, back and
  bounce are his, including the 1.525 in-out overshoot scale in `easeInOutBack`, the
  7.5625/2.75 bounce segments, and the endpoint exactness his v1.3 changelog introduced.

  Not his: the *spellings*. `power1`-`power4`, the `.in`/`.out`/`.inOut` suffixes and the
  `back.out(s)` parameter form are the authoring vocabulary GSAP added on top of the same
  curves; web-spark takes that vocabulary and, per docs/REVOLUTION.md §14, none of GSAP's
  runtime, whose licence forbids the kind of tool this project is.

  Attribution verified, not asserted: test/ease-laws.test.js transcribes the v1.5 equations
  in their original (t, b, c, d) form and holds every name in the table to them at 257
  points. That test is why the notices above are accurate rather than decorative.
